---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import RuntimeLayout from "../../components/RuntimeLayout.astro";
import { watershedCodeTheme } from "../../styles/code-theme";

const cfg = `import watershed/crdt_js

// Auto starts on WebRTC and attempts the relay in parallel — it never
// waits for one, and prefers it while healthy.
let config =
  crdt_js.config(
    room_id: "retro-board-9c2",
    replica_label: "ada",
    compatibility_tag: "retro-board/v1",
    root: crdt_js.pn_counter(),
    signaling: signaling,
  )
  |> crdt_js.with_transport_policy(crdt_js.Auto)
  |> crdt_js.with_sequencer(crdt_js.sequencer("wss://relay.example/room"))`;
---

<RuntimeLayout
  slug="p2p"
  description="How a watershed CRDT document runs on a WebRTC mesh without a sequencer. Signaling introduces peers, CRDT structures merge changes, and an optional relay provides durable storage."
>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Run a document without a sequencer</h2>
    <p>
      Most watershed documents use server order to resolve concurrent writes.
      A <strong>CRDT document</strong> does not require this order. Browsers find
      each other through WebRTC, edit while disconnected, and converge after
      they exchange state. Each eligible structure defines its own merge rule.
      The <a href={`/examples#clap_counter_lustre`}>clap counter example</a>
      runs without a sequencer. Two tabs can add claps at the same time without
      acknowledgments.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Signaling introduces peers</h2>
    <p>
      A signaling service exchanges offers, answers, and ICE candidates so that
      two browsers can connect directly. It can also authenticate room access.
      It does not order operations or store document state. After the data
      channel opens, signaling receives only opaque envelopes. A signaling
      outage prevents new peer discovery but does not affect connected peers.
    </p>
    <p>
      The full mesh supports up to eight peers. Every peer opens one data channel
      to every other peer. The ninth arrival receives <code>RoomFull</code>.
      This limit preserves the existing mesh. Applications supply their own STUN
      and TURN servers. watershed does not provide these servers. Host candidates
      connect only peers with no NAT between them.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Use structures with merge rules</h2>
    <p>
      Only CRDT channels can run without server order:
      <a href="/structures/counters#pn">PN counter</a>,
      <a href="/structures/sets#orset">OR-set</a>,
      the <a href="/structures/maps#ormap">OR-map</a>,
      <a href="/structures/sets#gset">G-set</a>,
      <a href="/structures/sets#twopset">2P-set</a>,
      <a href="/structures/sequences#sequence">SharedSequence</a>, and
      <a href="/structures/sequences#text">SharedText</a>. At
      <code>config</code> time, choose one of these structures as the root of a
      peer-to-peer document. The last-write-wins
      <a href="/structures/maps">SharedMap</a> cannot be the root.
    </p>
    <p>
      Structures that depend on server order or acknowledgment cannot run in
      this mode:
      <a href="/structures/maps">SharedMap</a>,
      <a href="/structures/counters">SharedCounter</a>, and
      <a href="/structures/maps#directory">SharedDirectory</a> resolve by
      sequence number; <a href="/structures/coordination#claims">claims</a>
      and <a href="/structures/coordination#registers">registers</a> depend on a
      sequence number; <a href="/structures/coordination#tasks">TaskManager</a>,
      <a href="/structures/coordination#pact">pact maps</a>, and{" "}
      <a href="/structures/coordination#ordered">ordered collections</a>{" "}
      depend on ordered membership. The OT-transformed{" "}
      <a href="/structures/transforms">json_ot and rich-text</a> channels
      transform against sequenced history. These structures do not have a merge
      function that can replace the sequencer.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>One document, three policies</h2>
    <p>
      A <code>CrdtDocument</code> chooses its transports once, at
      <code>config</code> time:
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={cfg} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      <strong><code>Auto</code></strong> is the default: the mesh and any
      configured relay connect independently. Readiness waits only for the mesh.
      After the relay connects, it becomes the durable delta path.
      <strong><code>P2pOnly</code></strong> opens no relay socket and ignores a
      configured relay. <strong><code>SequencedOnly</code></strong> uses no
      signaling or <code>RTCPeerConnection</code>. Readiness waits for the relay
      within a fixed deadline. All three policies use the same document and wire
      envelopes, so each policy can import a snapshot from another policy.
    </p>
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>The document continues after a relay disconnects.</strong> When
        the primary relay connection drops, the document reports the failover,
        sends its pending digest to the mesh, requests peer state, and continues
        over WebRTC. Public handles and converged state remain unchanged.
        <code>Auto</code> readiness does not depend on the relay.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>An optional durable relay</h2>
    <p>
      A relay that uses <code>crdt_relay_v1</code> provides durable storage and
      fan-out. It records a diagnostic order, broadcasts accepted data, and
      returns stored state on request. It does not merge data, decode kernel
      payloads, or select a winning replica. Connected replicas perform the
      merge. Without a relay, connected replicas hold the room state.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-out">
    <span class="annot">In short</span>
    <p>
      A CRDT document uses merge rules instead of server order. It can run on a
      WebRTC mesh without a sequencer. Signaling introduces peers, eligible
      structures merge changes, and an optional relay provides durability and
      wider access without changing document semantics.
    </p>
  </div>
</RuntimeLayout>
